\subsection{What a complete certificate must contain}
A reproducible certificate must identify its mathematical claim and a witness,
not just a green test count. At minimum its record needs the following.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em]
\item \textbf{Domain and quantifiers:} physical and bias family, fixed parameter
ancestry, H18/A21 mode and transition coverage, initial-state/coordinate domain,
word length, event phases, and the accepted magnetic-service hypothesis.
\item \textbf{Actual operands:} full covariance and cross terms, applied
$R_S,R_a,R_m$, tuning parameters, exact source forcing, gain masks, repairs,
projection and reset branches. Candidate or requested $R_S$ is not the
actually committed value used by a measurement.
\item \textbf{Verified inequalities:} storage positivity/coercivity, strict
word margin, disturbance gains, every-prefix retention and any chart or
arithmetic-domain bounds. Strictness must survive the complete rounding
budget, not merely a displayed decimal approximation.
\item \textbf{Coverage and arithmetic:} justification that cells cover every
admitted branch with shared source variables; outward rounding or exact
arithmetic for the checker; target compiler/library and floating-point
execution premises; explicit handling of rejected or nonfinite outcomes.
\item \textbf{Provenance and independent checking:} source revision/hashes,
parameters, generator/checker commands, retained machine-readable report,
limiting direction or counterexample, and negative tests that reject stale,
incomplete or inconsistent evidence.
\end{enumerate}
A rational identity test and a source-coverage gate answer different questions.
A successful checker can correctly verify a report whose \emph{theorem gate is
false}. Likewise, a positive local pivot or a closed library row may remain
conditional on a whole-firmware premise that has not been established.
\studysource{tools/stability/ou3_alt_contraction/finite_master_guard.py}
\studysource{tools/ou_evidence_contract.py}

\subsection{Finite precision is part of the theorem}
The target graph must account for binary32 inputs and casts, fused and
unfused operations, underflow, trigonometric/normalization branches,
exp/log/sqrt implementations, Eigen decisions, solves, Joseph/reset defects,
and the covariance actually stored. For example, the target arithmetic
components retain the execution premise \texttt{FCR.RM=0}; an ISA reset that
leaves that register undefined, followed by no observed writer, is not a proof
that round-to-nearest was selected. Component correspondence and full-firmware
initialization/preservation are separate obligations.

Similarly, saturating integer counters can be safe on every finite prefix
without proving that floating clocks advance indefinitely. A long finite
clock lookup does not establish $t_j\to\infty$, and a finite covariance audit
does not establish permanent membership in the finite-SPD domain. The study
must not convert these missing execution proofs into silently excluded
histories.
